WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.292
CARTER: If you're working on
Kubernetes in a production

00:00:02.292 --> 00:00:04.470
environment, especially on
a larger project or team,

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.180
you've probably run
into namespaces.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.680
We haven't talked
about them before,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.100
but today, we change that.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.070
Hello, Caslyn.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.170
Hello, Caslyn?

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.900
Hmm, Caslyn isn't in this room.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.640
Maybe I'll go into another one.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.260
Hello, Caslyn?

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.220
CASLYN: Oh, hi, Carter.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.592
What are you doing here?

00:00:23.592 --> 00:00:25.550
CARTER: I came to talk
to you about namespaces,

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.540
but I don't think I
could reach you before.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.690
CASLYN: That's kind
of ironic, actually.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.689
Namespaces are a tool to keep
Kubernetes objects separated,

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:33.590
as well.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:35.060
If you're in the
wrong space, you

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.137
won't be able to communicate
with your objects.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:38.720
CARTER: I could see
that being useful.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.012
Kind of like how containers
allow multiple applications

00:00:41.012 --> 00:00:43.430
to share one operating
system, namespaces

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.580
allow multiple Kubernetes
objects to share one cluster.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.180
CASLYN: Right, imagining it
as a sort of virtual cluster

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.810
makes a lot of sense, because
a namespace groups and isolates

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.450
Kubernetes objects across nodes.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.990
And it's not quite as strict
as container isolation,

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.900
because it's still possible to
access Kubernetes objects that

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.670
are in other namespaces.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:05.970
CARTER: That's good to know.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.060
So a namespace is
a virtual cluster

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.912
that's used to make it easier
for large teams to work.

00:01:10.912 --> 00:01:11.870
I've seen these before.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.537
I was working on a project where
the test and production objects

00:01:14.537 --> 00:01:15.740
were in separate namespaces.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.610
CASLYN: Sure, I've seen that.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.670
Another benefit of
namespaces comes in

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.530
with setting permissions.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.832
CARTER: Oh, yeah, I remember.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:25.040
The admin set it up so that
only certain people could

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.020
push a build to
production, but anyone

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.415
could push a build to test.

00:01:28.415 --> 00:01:30.040
CASLYN: They would
have done that using

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.950
Kubernetes role-based
access controls, or RBAC.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.130
Using RBAC, an administrator
can give each user

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.380
a role, which sets
their permissions

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.200
inside a specific namespace.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.243
CARTER: That makes sense.

00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:45.660
So the people on
my team must have

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.480
had roles that gave
them access to test,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.980
but only a few
people had roles that

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.450
gave them access to production.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.260
CASLYN: Right.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.080
Let's talk more about how to set
up the namespaces themselves.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.770
We can cover RBAC, or
role-based access controls,

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.960
in a future episode.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.110
CARTER: Sounds good to me.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.590
So how do I get started
using namespaces?

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.630
CASLYN: Technically,
you already are.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.870
Kubernetes starts with
four initial namespaces.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.820
You can check them out with the
kubectl get namespace command.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.250
CARTER: According to
the docs, kube-system

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.100
is for objects created
by the Kubernetes system.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.310
kube-public is mostly reserved
for cluster usage in case

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.740
some resources should be
visible and readable publicly

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.480
throughout the whole cluster.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.273
kube-node-lease is for the
lease objects associated

00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:32.190
with each node, which
improves the performance

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.590
of the node heartbeats
as a cluster scales.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.900
And the default
namespace is for objects

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.790
with no other namespace.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.160
It's like one of those bottom
drawers in the kitchen--

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.830
just throw anything in there.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.770
CASLYN: You can get more info
about any of these namespaces

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.770
using the kubectl get
and describe commands.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.440
For instance, you'll see
that describing the default

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.900
namespace returns
something like this.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.890
Note that these details
show both resource quota,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.400
if present, as well as
resource limit ranges.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.790
CARTER: Resource quotas
and resource limits?

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.820
CASLYN: Resource quota tracks
aggregate usage of resources

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.160
in the namespace and
allows cluster operators

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.320
to define hard
resource usage limits

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.450
that a namespace may consume.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.780
A limit range defines minimum
and maximum constraints

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.390
on the amount of resources
a single entity can

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.352
consume in a namespace.

00:03:23.352 --> 00:03:24.810
CARTER: That seems
useful if I need

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.990
to make sure resource usage is
staying within certain bounds.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.210
CASLYN: Yep, resource
quotas and limits

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.370
are an important tool
for administrators.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.680
CARTER: Since namespaces
are Kubernetes objects,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.250
I bet I can interact with
them using the kubectl create

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.370
and delete commands.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.080
CASLYN: Yes.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.940
One thing that's
a little different

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.660
is that since namespaces hold
other Kubernetes objects,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.570
if you want to get, create,
delete, or otherwise

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.670
operate on objects
in a namespace,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:55.240
you must specify that with
the --namespaces flag.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.150
CARTER: It seems like
a lot of extra typing,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.510
but I guess the shorthand
-n isn't all that bad.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.420
CASLYN: We can actually shorten
"deployment" here to "deploy,"

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.210
too.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.420
There are a lot of
convenient shorthand versions

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.808
of commands in Kubernetes.

00:04:07.808 --> 00:04:09.600
CARTER: That is
convenient, though it still

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.610
seems annoying to have
to type the namespace I'm

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.840
working in to every command.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.065
CASLYN: Well, you're in luck.

00:04:15.065 --> 00:04:16.440
If you're going
to be working out

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.300
of the same namespace
for a while,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.940
there's a way to set
kubectl to automatically use

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.480
that namespace for all
subsequent kubectl commands.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.340
To do that, you would use
kubectl config set-context.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.277
CARTER: Now that sounds handy.

00:04:31.277 --> 00:04:33.360
CASLYN: So I think that's
about it for the basics.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.950
You should have just
about everything

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.560
you need to know to
understand namespaces in case

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.490
you see them in the workplace.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:40.530
CARTER: I think so.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.180
Let me see if I've
got this right.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.220
A namespace is a
virtual cluster that

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.770
lets multiple groups of
users share a cluster.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.680
Each one has its own resources,
policies, and constraints,

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.690
meaning only people or
objects in my namespace

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:55.110
will see my pilot services,
and other Kubernetes objects.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.840
Also, I can define rules for
who can do what in my namespace,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.060
and I can enforce
how many resources

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.430
can be consumed by
objects in my namespace

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.590
using resource
limits and quotas.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.700
CASLYN: Exactly.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.250
Namespaces are a very
useful tool for everything

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.380
you just mentioned and more.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.240
CARTER: Wow, many
thanks to Caslyn

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.190
for sharing knowledge
about namespaces.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.160
For more information about
how, when, and why to use them,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.420
please check out the
documentation link below.

